  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy and cool conditions expected for\nHalloween.\n\nA cooler day is expected today as the post-frontal \nair mass moves in. Aloft, 925 mb temperatures cool to around 0C \nand NW winds continue, yet weaken with high pressure building \nin. Highs on Halloween will only reach the 50s as a result. \nSkies clear, and winds falling to or near calm Halloween night \nwill allow for radiational cooling, dropping lows to the 20s and\nlow 30s for much of the region. As is normally the case, the \ncoldest readings will be found across the far interior where \nlows fall to near 20 degrees. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure maintains quiet weather through \nat least Thursday with warmer than average highs in the 60s.\n\nHigh pressure will remain firmly in control of southern New \nEngland's weather clear through at least mid to late week as this \nstretch of pleasant weather continues. A large high moving in from \nNew York will initially move overhead on Sunday, replaced by another \ndropping south from Canada into Maine mid week. A weak trough will \npass over the Canadian maritimes around Tuesday bringing the \npotential for some showers to northern New England but lack of \nmoisture and dynamics over SNE will keep us dry. The next chance for \na pattern change comes around late Thursday/Friday when a mid level \ntrough may dig into the mid-Atlantic, but that is not a certainty at \nthis point. EPS and GEFS ensembles paint a 70-90% chance of PWATS \nclimbing above 1\" Thursday-Saturday next week. Run to run \nvariability shows less of a signal for QPF with the latest members \nshowing probs of >0.5\" of 24 hr QPF falling from 60-70% for the 12z \nruns to 30-50%. In the mean time, high pressure maintains a milder \nthan average airmass overhead leading to comfortable highs in the \n60s all week. Winds should be light until Friday when our passing \nlow would bring a period of gusty winds.",
